multichoice[20]{tags,text,exhibit,correct,w1,w2,w3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:ethics","A security consultant learns that a client plans a legal action that would seriously harm the public. According to the ISC2 Code of Professional Ethics, which canon takes priority when canons conflict?",,"Protect society, the common good, necessary public trust and confidence, and the infrastructure","Act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly, and legally","Provide diligent and competent service to principals","Advance and protect the profession","The canons are applied in order, and the first is to protect society, the common good, public trust and the infrastructure. Honorable and legal conduct, service to principals and the profession follow in that order.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:ai-ethics","A bank's AI model denies loans, and an audit shows that applicants from one neighborhood are refused far more often with no business justification. Which ethical concern does this primarily raise?",,"Bias and fairness, which calls for reviewing the training data and the outcomes","Availability, which calls for a hot recovery site","Non-repudiation, which calls for digital signatures on every decision","Data remanence, which calls for media sanitization","Unjustified differences between groups are a sign of bias in data or design, so fairness reviews, transparency and human oversight are the response. Availability, non-repudiation and remanence are unrelated to the pattern.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:risk","A server is worth $200,000. A flood would destroy 25 percent of its value and is expected once every 5 years. What is the annualized loss expectancy (ALE)?",,"$10,000","$50,000","$40,000","$2,000","SLE is asset value times exposure factor: $200,000 x 0.25 = $50,000. ARO is 1/5 = 0.2, so ALE = $50,000 x 0.2 = $10,000. $50,000 is the SLE, and the other values do not follow from the formulas.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:classification","Who is ultimately responsible for deciding the classification of a data set?",,"The data owner","The data custodian","The system administrator","The security auditor","The owner is accountable for the data and decides its classification and who may access it. The custodian applies the protection that the owner requires, and administrators and auditors do not set classification.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.4|objective:isc2:cissp:2.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:media-sanitization","A company retires solid-state drives that held sensitive data. Which method most reliably prevents recovery of the data?",,"Physical destruction of the drives","Degaussing the drives","Deleting the files and emptying the recycle bin","Running a quick format","SSDs store data in flash cells, so degaussing has no effect on them, and deleting or quick formatting leaves the data in place. Physical destruction, or a verified cryptographic erase, is reliable.",3,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:data-states","Which control best protects data while it is in use?",,"Processing in a trusted execution environment such as a secure enclave, or encrypting memory","TLS between the client and the server","Full-disk encryption on the server","Encrypting the backup tapes","Data in use is held unencrypted in memory while it is processed, so protections focus on enclaves, memory encryption and access control. TLS covers data in transit, and disk and tape encryption cover data at rest.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:security-models","A military system enforces no read up and no write down to protect confidentiality. Which security model does this describe?",,"Bell-LaPadula","Biba","Clark-Wilson","Brewer-Nash","Bell-LaPadula protects confidentiality with the simple security property (no read up) and the star property (no write down). Biba protects integrity with the reverse rules, Clark-Wilson uses well-formed transactions, and Brewer-Nash prevents conflicts of interest.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:post-quantum","Which concern drives the move to post-quantum cryptography?",,"A large quantum computer could break widely used public-key algorithms such as RSA and ECC, and data captured today could be decrypted later","Quantum computers make symmetric encryption with long keys useless","Hash functions become reversible on quantum hardware","TLS 1.3 cannot run on networks that use quantum key distribution","Shor's algorithm threatens RSA and elliptic-curve cryptography, so long-lived data that is captured now is at risk, which is the harvest now, decrypt later concern. Symmetric ciphers and hashes are affected much less, mainly by needing larger sizes.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.6|objective:cisco:350-901:3.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:tls|topic:pki","A web server presents only its own certificate, and some clients report that the local issuer certificate cannot be found. What is the best fix?",,"Configure the server to send the full chain, including the intermediate CA certificate","Disable certificate validation on the clients","Replace the CA-signed certificate with a self-signed certificate","Reissue the client certificates","Clients build the path from the server certificate to a trusted root, and they can do so only if the server presents the intermediate certificate. Disabling validation or using a self-signed certificate removes the trust that PKI provides.",4,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:osi","Which OSI layer provides end-to-end delivery and the TCP and UDP port numbers?",,"Transport (layer 4)","Network (layer 3)","Data link (layer 2)","Session (layer 5)","The transport layer carries TCP and UDP and uses ports to deliver data to the right process. The network layer handles IP addressing and routing, and the data link layer handles frames and MAC addresses.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|objective:cisco:300-435:3.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:segmentation|topic:zero-trust","A data center team wants traffic between workloads in the same subnet to be filtered per workload. Which approach fits best?",,"Micro-segmentation with distributed firewall policy enforced at each workload","A larger perimeter firewall at the Internet edge","One flat VLAN with a stronger password policy","A separate air-gapped network for every workload","East-west traffic never reaches the perimeter, so per-workload policy is enforced close to the workload, which is micro-segmentation. A perimeter firewall cannot see it, and air-gapping every workload is impractical.",3,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:access-control","A file's access is decided by its classification label and the user's clearance, set by a central authority, and owners cannot change it. Which model is this?",,"Mandatory access control (MAC)","Discretionary access control (DAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC)","Rule-based access control","In MAC the system compares labels and clearances that the owner cannot change. DAC lets the owner decide, RBAC grants permissions through roles, and rule-based control applies global rules such as time of day.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:provisioning","An employee moves from accounting to marketing and keeps the old access. What is this called, and which control addresses it?",,"Privilege creep, addressed by access recertification and a review at every role change","A separation of duties violation, addressed by job rotation","Session hijacking, addressed by shorter session timeouts","Shadow IT, addressed by data loss prevention","Access that accumulates as people change roles is privilege creep. Reviewing access when roles change and recertifying it periodically removes what is no longer needed.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:passkeys|topic:fido","Which property makes FIDO2 and WebAuthn passkeys resistant to phishing?",,"The credential is bound to the legitimate site's origin, so a lookalike site cannot use it","The passkey is a long random password kept in a password manager","A code is sent by SMS and must be typed into the page","The private key is shared with the relying party to compare","A passkey is created for a specific origin, and the authenticator will not answer a request from a different site. Passwords and codes can be typed into a fake page, and the private key never leaves the authenticator.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:pentest","Which activity actively tries to exploit weaknesses to show their impact, under written authorization?",,"Penetration testing","Vulnerability scanning","Log review","Compliance checking","A penetration test goes beyond finding weaknesses and attempts to exploit them within an agreed scope. A scan only identifies weaknesses, and log reviews and compliance checks do not exploit anything.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:audit","A cloud provider wants to give customers assurance that its controls operated effectively over a period of time. Which report fits best?",,"SOC 2 Type II","SOC 2 Type I","SOC 1 Type I","A vulnerability scan report","A Type II report tests the operating effectiveness of controls over a period. A Type I report describes the design of controls at a single point in time, SOC 1 addresses financial reporting, and a scan is not an attestation.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:forensics","Which practice best preserves the admissibility of digital evidence?",,"Documenting a chain of custody and working from a forensic copy verified with a hash","Booting the original system to look for the relevant files","Letting the suspect's administrator copy the files","Deleting temporary files before imaging to save space","Admissibility depends on showing that evidence was handled by known people and was not altered. A verified forensic copy lets analysts work without touching the original. The other actions change or contaminate the evidence.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.2|objective:cisco:350-901:3.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:siem|topic:logging","A SIEM receives syslog from hundreds of devices, but analysts cannot correlate events across sources. Which log management practice most directly fixes this?",,"Synchronize clocks with NTP and normalize timestamps and fields across sources","Keep the logs for ten years","Set every device to debug severity","Store the logs only on each device","Correlation needs events from different sources to line up in time and share a common format. Longer retention, more verbose logging and local storage do not make events comparable.",4,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.5|objective:cisco:350-901:3.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:secure-coding|topic:sql-injection","A web form builds a database query by concatenating user input. Which control best prevents SQL injection?",,"Parameterized queries together with server-side input validation","JavaScript validation in the browser only","Encrypting the database connection with TLS","Hiding the names of the form fields","Parameterized queries keep input as data and never as part of the SQL text, and the server must validate input because client checks can be bypassed. TLS protects the connection but not the query, and hiding field names does nothing.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.2|objective:cisco:350-901:2.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cicd|topic:sast","Where in a CI/CD pipeline should static application security testing (SAST) run so that developers learn about flaws early?",,"On each commit or merge request, before the code is built and deployed","Only in production after the release","Once a year before the audit","After deployment, as part of incident response","SAST analyzes source code without running it, so it can run as soon as code is committed. Running it late makes fixes more expensive and lets flaws reach production.",2,2

multiselect[20]{tags,text,exhibit,c1,c2,c3,w1,w2,w3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:non-repudiation","Which two controls primarily support non-repudiation? (Choose two.)",,"Digital signatures created with the sender's private key","Audit logs tied to unique, strongly authenticated user IDs",,"Symmetric encryption with a shared key","A hash of the message without a key","RAID mirroring of the log disk","Non-repudiation means that a person cannot credibly deny an action. Signatures and attributable logs link actions to one identity. A shared key, an unkeyed hash and mirroring cannot show who acted.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:gdpr|topic:privacy","Which two statements about the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) are correct? (Choose two.)",,"It can apply to organizations outside the EU that process personal data of people in the EU","It generally requires notifying the supervisory authority of a personal data breach within 72 hours",,"It applies only to companies headquartered in the EU","It prohibits every transfer of personal data outside the EU","It covers only health data","GDPR has extraterritorial reach, and a breach notification is generally due within 72 hours of becoming aware of it. Transfers outside the EU are allowed with safeguards, and the regulation covers all personal data.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:due-diligence","Which two actions demonstrate due diligence rather than due care? (Choose two.)",,"Researching a vendor's security posture before signing a contract","Performing a risk assessment before selecting controls",,"Applying patches on a defined schedule","Enforcing the password policy on all accounts","Reviewing logs every day","Due diligence is the investigation done before acting, and due care is acting prudently on what was learned. Patching, enforcing policy and reviewing logs are ongoing acts of due care.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:data-roles","Which two statements about data roles are correct? (Choose two.)",,"The data owner is accountable for classification and decides who may access the data","A data processor handles personal data on behalf of the data controller",,"The data custodian decides the classification label","The data subject is the administrator who runs the database","The data controller acts only on the instructions of the processor","The owner decides classification and access, and the custodian carries out the protection. Under privacy law the processor works for the controller, and the data subject is the person the data describes.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cnapp|topic:dlp|topic:casb","Which three statements about data protection methods are correct? (Choose three.)",,"DLP detects and blocks or reports policy violations for data in motion, at rest or in use","A CASB enforces policy and provides visibility between users and cloud services","A CNAPP combines cloud workload and posture protection, such as misconfiguration and vulnerability detection, in one platform","DRM encrypts site-to-site network traffic","DLP removes the need to classify data","A CASB is a tool for sanitizing media","DLP, CASB and CNAPP protect data in and around cloud and endpoint environments. DRM controls how content can be used, DLP depends on classification to know what to protect, and sanitizing media is unrelated to a CASB.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:secure-design","Which two statements correctly describe secure design principles? (Choose two.)",,"Least privilege gives subjects only the access needed for their tasks","Failing securely leaves the system in a secure state, for example by denying access",,"Defense in depth relies on one very strong control","Secure defaults means that features ship enabled and users turn off what they do not need","Separation of duties lets one person complete a sensitive process from start to finish","Least privilege and fail-secure behavior are core principles. Defense in depth uses several layers, secure defaults means safe settings out of the box, and separation of duties splits sensitive steps among people.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.7|objective:cisco:350-901:4.2|objective:cisco:300-435:5.3|objective:cisco:300-635:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:ai-security","Which two are AI-related threats to a security architecture? (Choose two.)",,"Adversarial inputs crafted to make a model misclassify","Model poisoning by corrupting the training data",,"Rainbow table lookups","Cold boot attacks on memory","Bluejacking of nearby phones","Attackers can fool a model at run time with adversarial inputs, or corrupt it earlier by poisoning its training data. Rainbow tables, cold boot attacks and bluejacking are older attacks that are not specific to AI.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.6|objective:isc2:cissp:3.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:key-management|topic:hsm","Which three practices belong to good cryptographic key management? (Choose three.)",,"Generate keys with a cryptographically secure random source","Rotate keys on a schedule and after a suspected compromise","Keep keys apart from the data they protect, for example in an HSM or a key management service","Reuse one key for every application to keep things simple","Embed keys in source code so that they are always available","Store keys in the same database table as the encrypted data","Strong randomness, rotation and separation from the protected data, ideally in an HSM or KMS, limit the damage of a compromise. Reusing keys, embedding them in code or storing them beside the data all increase exposure.",3,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:secure-protocols","Which two protocols provide confidentiality for data in transit? (Choose two.)",,"SSH","TLS",,"Telnet","FTP","SNMPv1","SSH and TLS encrypt the session. Telnet, FTP and SNMPv1 send credentials and data in clear text.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.2|objective:cisco:300-435:3.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:nac","Which two functions does a network access control (NAC) system perform? (Choose two.)",,"Checks the posture of a device before granting access to the network","Assigns the device to a VLAN or policy based on identity and compliance",,"Encrypts every packet end to end between all hosts","Replaces DNS name resolution","Captures every packet from all users for later review","NAC decides whether and how a device may connect, using identity and posture. It does not provide end-to-end encryption, name resolution or full packet capture.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:vpn|topic:remote-access","Which two statements about secure remote access are correct? (Choose two.)",,"A split-tunnel VPN sends only corporate traffic through the tunnel and the rest directly to the Internet","Multifactor authentication reduces the risk of stolen credentials for remote access",,"A full-tunnel VPN lets the client bypass corporate filtering","Remote administration should use Telnet inside the VPN","A VPN makes endpoint security unnecessary","Split tunneling and full tunneling differ in which traffic uses the tunnel, and MFA protects against stolen credentials. Full tunnel sends everything through corporate controls, Telnet is unencrypted, and a VPN does not secure the endpoint itself.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:saml|topic:federation","Which two statements about SAML federation are correct? (Choose two.)",,"The identity provider authenticates the user and issues an assertion","The service provider trusts assertions from the identity provider and grants access",,"The service provider stores the user's password","SAML assertions are encrypted with the user's password","Federation requires every service to keep its own user database","In SAML the identity provider authenticates and asserts identity, and the service provider relies on that assertion. The service provider does not see the password, and federation exists to avoid separate user databases.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:kerberos","Which two statements about Kerberos are correct? (Choose two.)",,"A Key Distribution Center issues tickets, so passwords are not sent to each service","Synchronized clocks are needed to limit replay of tickets",,"The user's password is sent to every service","It uses only public-key cryptography","It does not need a trusted third party","Kerberos relies on a trusted KDC that issues tickets, and time stamps limit replay, so clocks must be close. It uses mostly symmetric cryptography, and passwords are not sent to services.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:dast|topic:testing","Which two techniques test the security of a running web application? (Choose two.)",,"Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Interface testing of the API with malformed or unexpected input",,"Reading the source code line by line in a manual code review","Running static analysis (SAST) on the repository","Checking the project against the coding standard document","DAST and interface testing exercise the application while it runs. Code review, SAST and standards checks examine code or documents without running the application.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:kri","Which two are examples of key risk indicators? (Choose two.)",,"The number of critical vulnerabilities left unpatched for more than 30 days","The percentage of privileged accounts without multifactor authentication",,"The number of firewall rules created this month","Total spending on security tools","The number of awareness slides produced","Key risk indicators signal growing exposure, such as overdue patches and weak authentication. Activity counts and spending describe effort, not risk.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:incident-response","Which two actions are most appropriate immediately after confirming a malware outbreak on several workstations? (Choose two.)",,"Isolate the affected hosts from the network","Preserve volatile evidence and relevant logs",,"Reimage all the machines before collecting any evidence","Announce the incident publicly before assessing the impact","Delete the malware samples","Containment stops the spread, and preserving evidence supports analysis and legal needs. Reimaging first destroys evidence, premature announcements are risky, and deleting samples removes material for analysis.",3,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.7|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:ids|topic:ips","Which two statements about IDS and IPS are correct? (Choose two.)",,"An IPS sits inline and can block malicious traffic","An IDS monitors a copy of the traffic and raises alerts",,"An IDS sits inline and drops malicious packets","An IPS only logs and never blocks","Both detect only zero-day attacks","An IPS is inline and can prevent, while an IDS observes and alerts. Both rely on signatures, anomalies or behavior and are not limited to zero-day attacks.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.10|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:backup|topic:ransomware","Which three characteristics make a backup strategy resilient to ransomware? (Choose three.)",,"At least one copy is immutable or offline","Copies are stored in a separate location and restores are tested","Backup systems use separate credentials protected by multifactor authentication","All backups are mounted read-write on the production network","Restores are tested only after an incident","Backup systems use the same credentials as domain administrators","Ransomware tries to encrypt or delete backups, so copies that cannot be changed, kept apart and protected by separate credentials survive. Tested restores prove that the backups work. Shared credentials and always-mounted backups expose them.",3,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:sca|topic:sbom","Which two actions assess the security impact of open source components? (Choose two.)",,"Use software composition analysis to find known vulnerabilities and license issues in dependencies","Maintain a software bill of materials (SBOM) for each application",,"Trust the component because it is popular","Assume that open source has no vulnerabilities because many people read it","Skip the assessment when the component is used only internally","SCA and an SBOM show what is in the software and which components are vulnerable. Popularity, public source code or internal use do not remove the need for assessment.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:devsecops|topic:shift-left","Which two statements about DevSecOps and shift left are correct? (Choose two.)",,"Shift left moves security activities such as threat modeling and testing earlier in the lifecycle","DevSecOps builds automated security testing into the CI/CD pipeline",,"Security reviews are done once at the end of the project","DevSecOps replaces the need for secure coding standards","Shift left means delaying testing until after release","Shifting left finds flaws when they are cheaper to fix, and DevSecOps automates checks in the pipeline. A single late review, dropping standards or testing after release do the opposite.",2,2

match[20]{tags,text,exhibit,p1,r1,p2,r2,p3,r3,p4,r4,p5,r5,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:controls","Match each type of control to an example.",,"Preventive","A firewall rule that blocks a port","Detective","An alert raised by an intrusion detection system","Corrective","Restoring a system from backup after an incident","Deterrent","A warning banner that states monitoring and penalties","Compensating","An extra review used when a primary control is not practical","Preventive controls stop events, detective controls find them, corrective controls repair the damage, deterrent controls discourage action, and compensating controls make up for a missing control.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:policy","Match each security document to its description.",,"Policy","High-level statement of management intent","Standard","Mandatory, specific requirement","Procedure","Step-by-step instructions for a task","Guideline","Recommended, discretionary advice","Baseline","Minimum security configuration for a type of system","Policies set direction, standards make it specific and mandatory, procedures give steps, guidelines advise, and baselines define the minimum configuration.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:investigations","Match each investigation type to its description.",,"Administrative","Internal inquiry into a violation of company policy","Criminal","Requires proof beyond a reasonable doubt","Civil","Decided on the preponderance of the evidence","Regulatory","Checks compliance with rules set by a government or industry body",,,"Each type has its own purpose and burden of proof: policy violations, criminal law with the highest standard, civil disputes with a lower standard, and regulatory compliance.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:data-lifecycle","Match each data lifecycle term to its description.",,"Data remanence","Residual data that remains on media after deletion","Data retention","How long data must be kept to meet legal or business needs","Data destruction","Making data unrecoverable at the end of its life","Data location","Where data is stored, which affects the laws that apply","Data maintenance","Keeping data accurate, current and protected during use","The terms describe stages and concerns across the life of data: what is left behind, how long to keep it, how to destroy it, where it sits and how it is kept in good order.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:media-sanitization","Match each media sanitization term to its description.",,"Clear","Overwriting so that normal tools cannot recover the data","Purge","Stronger techniques, such as cryptographic erase, that make recovery infeasible even in a lab","Destroy","Shredding, pulverizing or incinerating the media","Cryptographic erase","Destroying the encryption key so that the ciphertext is unreadable","Degauss","Using a strong magnetic field to erase magnetic media","The levels of sanitization increase from clear to purge to destroy. Cryptographic erase and degaussing are specific purge techniques.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:security-models","Match each security model to its focus.",,"Bell-LaPadula","Confidentiality: no read up, no write down","Biba","Integrity: no read down, no write up","Clark-Wilson","Integrity through well-formed transactions and separation of duties","Brewer-Nash","Prevents conflicts of interest, also called the Chinese Wall","Graham-Denning","Rules for creating and deleting subjects and objects and granting rights","Each model addresses a specific problem: confidentiality, integrity in two forms, conflicts of interest, and the secure creation and deletion of subjects and objects.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.7|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cryptanalysis","Match each attack to its description.",,"Known plaintext","The attacker has plaintext and the matching ciphertext","Ciphertext only","The attacker has only encrypted data","Side-channel","Exploits physical leakage such as timing or power use","Fault injection","Induces errors to expose secrets","Pass the hash","Authenticates with a stolen password hash instead of the password","The first two differ in what the attacker holds, side-channel and fault injection attack the implementation, and pass the hash reuses a stolen credential.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:osi","Match each OSI layer to its function or example.",,"Physical","Cables, signals and bits","Data link","Frames and MAC addresses","Network","IP addressing and routing","Transport","TCP and UDP segments and ports","Application","Interfaces such as HTTP and DNS for applications","The layers build upward from bits to frames, packets, segments and application protocols.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|objective:cisco:300-435:3.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:segmentation","Match each segmentation term to its description.",,"Physical segmentation","Separate hardware, such as an air-gapped network","Logical segmentation","VLANs, VRFs or VPNs sharing the same hardware","Micro-segmentation","Per-workload policy enforced by distributed firewalls or overlays","Out-of-band management","Management traffic carried on a separate network","East-west traffic","Server-to-server traffic inside the data center","Segmentation can be physical, logical or per workload. Out-of-band management separates administration traffic, and east-west describes traffic that stays inside the data center.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:wireless","Match each wireless or cellular technology to its description.",,"Wi-Fi","IEEE 802.11 wireless LAN","Bluetooth","Short-range personal area network","Zigbee","Low-power mesh network often used for IoT","5G","Cellular network with features such as network slicing","Satellite","Wide coverage with high latency","The technologies differ in range, power, topology and latency, and so in their risks.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:access-control","Match each access control model to its description.",,"RBAC","Permissions are assigned to roles and users receive roles","ABAC","Decisions evaluate attributes of the subject, object and environment","MAC","Labels and clearances enforced by the system","DAC","The owner decides who has access","Rule-based","Global rules, such as time of day, apply to all requests","The models differ in who decides and on what basis: roles, attributes, labels, owners or fixed rules.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:aaa","Match each term to its meaning.",,"Identification","Claiming an identity","Authentication","Proving the claimed identity","Authorization","Determining what the identity may do","Accounting","Recording what the identity did","Session management","Controlling the lifetime, timeout and tokens of an authenticated session","Access control proceeds from claiming identity to proving it, deciding permissions and recording actions, with session management governing how long it lasts.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:federation","Match each protocol to its purpose.",,"SAML","XML assertions for web single sign-on between organizations","OAuth 2.0","Delegated authorization with access tokens","OpenID Connect","Authentication layer on OAuth 2.0 that returns an ID token","RADIUS","AAA for network access","TACACS+","AAA for device administration with separate authentication, authorization and accounting","The first three are used for web and API identity, and RADIUS and TACACS+ provide AAA for network and device access.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:pentest","Match each test approach to its description.",,"Black box","The tester has no knowledge of internals","White box","The tester has full knowledge, including source code","Gray box","The tester has partial knowledge","Red team","Simulates an adversary, often without warning the defenders","Purple team","Red and blue teams collaborate to improve detection","The box colors describe how much the tester knows, and the team colors describe the structure of the exercise.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:audit","Match each assessment term to its description.",,"Internal audit","Performed by the organization's own staff","External audit","Performed by an independent party for stakeholders","Third-party assessment","Examines a supplier's controls from outside the enterprise","Compliance check","Verifies conformity with a standard or regulation","Synthetic transaction","Scripted transaction used to measure behavior and availability","Audits differ in who performs them and why, and synthetic transactions provide active measurement of a service.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.10|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:recovery-sites","Match each recovery site type to its description.",,"Hot site","Fully equipped and ready within hours","Warm site","Hardware present, with data restored from backups, ready in days","Cold site","Space and utilities only, with the longest recovery time","Mobile site","Transportable trailer with equipment","Reciprocal agreement","Two organizations agree to host each other after a disaster","Sites trade cost for recovery speed, from hot to cold, and mobile sites and reciprocal agreements are alternatives.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.12|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:disaster-recovery","Match each disaster recovery test to its description.",,"Read-through (tabletop)","The team reviews the plan documents","Walkthrough","Participants talk through the steps in a meeting","Simulation","A scenario is acted out to test responses","Parallel test","The recovery site is brought up while production continues","Full interruption","Production is shut down and operations move to the recovery site","Tests increase in realism and risk from reading the plan to a full interruption.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.7|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:detection","Match each detection or response technology to its description.",,"Honeypot","Decoy system that attracts and studies attackers","Sandbox","Isolated environment for running suspicious code","EDR","Endpoint telemetry, detection and response","SIEM","Collects and correlates logs from many sources","SOAR","Automates response with playbooks","Each technology has a role: deception, isolation, endpoint visibility, central correlation and automated response.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:sdlc","Match each development approach or framework to its description.",,"Waterfall","Sequential phases, each completed before the next","Agile","Iterative work in short cycles with continuous feedback","DevOps","Collaboration of development and operations with automation","DevSecOps","Security integrated throughout the pipeline","SAMM","Open framework for measuring and improving software security practices","The approaches differ in how work is sequenced and how security is included, and SAMM measures the maturity of security practices.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:secure-coding","Match each coding weakness to its description.",,"SQL injection","Untrusted input changes a database query","Cross-site scripting","Injected script runs in another user's browser","Cross-site request forgery","An authenticated browser is tricked into sending an unwanted request","Buffer overflow","Data written past the end of a buffer corrupts memory","Insecure direct object reference","Changing an identifier reaches another user's data","These are common source-level weaknesses, each with a different cause and impact.",1,2

order[20]{tags,text,exhibit,s1,s2,s3,s4,s5,s6,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:nist-rmf","Place the main steps of the NIST Risk Management Framework, after preparation, in the correct order.",,"Categorize the system","Select the controls","Implement the controls","Assess the controls","Authorize the system","Monitor the controls","The framework moves from categorizing the system to selecting, implementing and assessing controls, authorizing operation and then monitoring continuously.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.7|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:bcp","Place the steps of developing a business continuity plan in the correct order.",,"Define the project scope and obtain management support","Perform a business impact analysis","Identify preventive controls and recovery strategies","Develop the plan","Test the plan","Maintain and update the plan","Scope and support come first, then the BIA decides priorities and recovery targets. Strategies feed the plan, which is tested and kept up to date.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:data-lifecycle","Place the stages of the data lifecycle in the correct order.",,"Create or collect","Store","Use","Share","Archive","Destroy","Data is created or collected, stored and used, shared, kept for the retention period in an archive, and finally destroyed.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:classification","Place the steps of a data classification program in the correct order.",,"Define the classification levels and criteria","Inventory the assets and assign owners","Owners classify the data","Apply the handling controls for each level","Review the classifications periodically",,"Levels and criteria come first, owners are identified so that they can classify, controls follow the label, and reviews keep it current.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.2|objective:isc2:cissp:2.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:media-sanitization","Place the steps of disposing of a storage device that held sensitive data in the correct order.",,"Identify the classification of the data on the device","Choose the sanitization method required by policy","Perform the sanitization","Verify that it worked","Record it with a certificate and chain of custody","Dispose of or reuse the device","The classification decides the method, which is carried out, verified and documented before the device leaves control.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.10|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:lifecycle","Place the early stages of the information system lifecycle in the correct order.",,"Stakeholder needs and requirements","Requirements analysis","Secure architectural design","Development and implementation","Integration","Verification and validation","The lifecycle starts with needs, then analysis and design, and continues with building, integrating and verifying before deployment.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:tls","Place the events of a simplified TLS 1.3 handshake in the correct order.",,"The client sends a ClientHello with its supported ciphers and key share","The server answers with a ServerHello, its key share and its certificate","The client verifies the certificate chain","Both sides derive the session keys","Application data flows encrypted",,"The hellos exchange parameters and key shares, the client checks the certificate, both derive keys, and only then is application data sent.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:ipsec","Place the stages of establishing an IPsec VPN in the correct order.",,"IKE phase 1 negotiates the IKE security association","The peers authenticate each other","IKE phase 2 negotiates the IPsec security associations","Traffic is protected with ESP",,,"The peers first build a secure channel and authenticate, then negotiate the IPsec parameters that protect the data traffic.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:osi","Place the steps of encapsulating data on a sending host in the correct order.",,"The application creates the data","The transport layer adds a TCP header to make a segment","The network layer adds an IP header to make a packet","The data link layer adds a header and trailer to make a frame","The physical layer transmits the bits",,"Data is wrapped in a new header at each layer as it moves down the stack, and only the final frame is sent as bits.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.2|objective:cisco:300-435:3.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:nac|topic:8021x","Place the events of 802.1X port-based access control in the correct order.",,"The supplicant connects and sends an EAPOL start","The authenticator requests the identity","The authentication server, usually RADIUS, verifies the credentials","The server returns Access-Accept with a VLAN or policy","The port is authorized",,"The device starts the exchange, the switch relays it to the RADIUS server, and the port opens only after the server accepts and returns the policy.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:provisioning","Place the stages of the access provisioning lifecycle in the correct order.",,"Request and approval","Provisioning","Periodic review and recertification","Modification when the role changes","Deprovisioning on departure",,"Access is requested and approved before it is provisioned, then reviewed and adjusted over time, and removed when the person leaves.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:identity","Place the stages of establishing and using an identity in the correct order.",,"Registration and identity proofing","Credential issuance","Authentication at login","A session is established with a token","An authorization decision is made for each request","The session ends by timeout or logoff","An identity is proven and given a credential before it can authenticate, and the session then carries authorization decisions until it ends.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:pentest","Place the phases of a penetration test in the correct order.",,"Planning and rules of engagement","Reconnaissance","Scanning and enumeration","Exploitation","Post-exploitation","Reporting","Written authorization and scope come first, then information is gathered and weaknesses are found and exploited. Results are reported at the end.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:remediation","Place the steps of handling test findings in the correct order.",,"Collect and validate the results","Prioritize by risk","Report to stakeholders","Remediate, or accept the risk through an exception","Retest to verify the fix",,"Findings are validated and prioritized before being reported. After remediation or a documented exception, a retest confirms the result.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:audit","Place the steps of a security audit in the correct order.",,"Define the scope and objectives","Plan and gather evidence","Test the controls","Report the findings","Track the remediation",,"An audit begins with its scope, collects evidence and tests controls, reports the findings and follows up on remediation.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:incident-response","Place the phases of incident response in the correct order.",,"Preparation","Detection and analysis","Containment","Eradication","Recovery","Lessons learned","Preparation comes before an incident. Once an incident is detected it is contained, the cause is removed, systems are restored, and the team learns from it.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:change-management","Place the steps of a change management process in the correct order.",,"A change request is submitted","Impact and risk are analyzed","The change board approves it","The change is implemented according to plan","The result is tested and verified","The change is documented and closed","A change is requested, assessed and approved before implementation. It is verified and documented afterwards.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:sdlc","Place the phases of a secure software development lifecycle in the correct order.",,"Requirements and threat modeling","Design","Development with secure coding","Testing with SAST and DAST","Deployment","Operations and maintenance","Security work starts with requirements and threat modeling, continues through design, coding and testing, and goes on after deployment.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.2|objective:cisco:350-901:2.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cicd","Place the stages of a CI/CD pipeline with security testing in the correct order.",,"A developer commits to the repository","The pipeline builds the code","SAST and dependency scans run","The application is deployed to test and DAST runs","The release is approved and deployed to production","Monitoring continues in production","Static checks run on the code before deployment, dynamic tests run on the deployed test system, and production deployment follows approval.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:acquisition","Place the steps of acquiring third-party software securely in the correct order.",,"Define the security requirements","Evaluate the vendor and the product","Require patching and breach notification in the contract","Test the product in a sandbox","Approve and deploy","Monitor and reassess","Requirements guide the evaluation and the contract, testing precedes approval, and monitoring continues after deployment.",2,3

slotfill[20]{tags,text,exhibit,a1,a2,a3,a4,a5,d1,d2,d3,explanation,bloom,difficulty}:
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:risk","Fill in each blank to complete the quantitative risk formulas.","```text\nSLE = AV x ⟦1⟧\nALE = SLE x ⟦2⟧\n```","EF","ARO",,,,"ALE","RTO","MTD","The single loss expectancy is the asset value times the exposure factor, and the annualized loss expectancy is the single loss expectancy times the annualized rate of occurrence. ALE, RTO and MTD are not factors of these formulas.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.7|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:bcp","Fill in each blank with the continuity metric that fits the definition.","```text\nThe ⟦1⟧ is the longest outage the business can tolerate for a process.\nThe ⟦2⟧ is the target time to restore the process and must be shorter than the first.\nThe ⟦3⟧ is the amount of data loss, measured in time, that the business accepts.\n```","MTD","RTO","RPO",,,"MTBF","SLA","ALE","The maximum tolerable downtime sets the limit, the recovery time objective is the restoration target within it, and the recovery point objective limits data loss. MTBF, SLA and ALE are different measures.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:1.10|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:threat-modeling|topic:stride","Fill in each blank with the property that the STRIDE threat violates.","```text\nSpoofing violates authentication.\nTampering violates ⟦1⟧.\nRepudiation violates ⟦2⟧.\nInformation disclosure violates ⟦3⟧.\n```","integrity","non-repudiation","confidentiality",,,"availability","authorization","privacy","Tampering changes data, which violates integrity, repudiation allows denial of an action, which violates non-repudiation, and information disclosure exposes data, which violates confidentiality.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:data-states","Fill in each blank with the control that best protects the data state.","```text\nData at rest: ⟦1⟧\nData in transit: ⟦2⟧\nData in use: ⟦3⟧\n```","Full-disk encryption","TLS","Secure enclave",,,"Degaussing","Hashing","Checksums","Disk encryption protects stored data, TLS protects data in motion, and an enclave protects data while it is processed. Degaussing, hashing and checksums do not protect these states.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:2.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:data-roles","Fill in each blank with the role that matches the description.","```text\nThe ⟦1⟧ is accountable for the data and sets its classification.\nThe ⟦2⟧ implements the controls day to day.\nThe ⟦3⟧ is the person the personal data is about.\n```","data owner","data custodian","data subject",,,"data processor","data controller","auditor","The owner decides, the custodian carries out the protection, and the data subject is the individual whom the data describes. A processor and a controller are privacy roles, and an auditor only reviews.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:security-models","Fill in each blank to complete the rules of the models.","```text\nBell-LaPadula simple security property: no read ⟦1⟧\nBell-LaPadula star property: no write ⟦2⟧\nBiba simple integrity axiom: no read ⟦3⟧\n```","up","down","down",,,"across","through","over","Bell-LaPadula prevents reading above one's clearance and writing below it. Biba protects integrity, so a subject must not read data of lower integrity. The other words are not used in these rules.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:cryptography","Fill in each blank with the cryptographic primitive that matches the description.","```text\n⟦1⟧ encryption uses one shared key.\n⟦2⟧ encryption uses a key pair.\nA ⟦3⟧ function produces a fixed-size digest.\n```","symmetric","asymmetric","hash",,,"stream","certificate","escrow","Symmetric encryption uses a single shared key, asymmetric encryption uses a public and a private key, and a hash function produces a fixed-size digest. A stream is a kind of cipher, and a certificate and escrow are not primitives.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:3.9|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:fire-suppression","Fill in each blank to describe the sprinkler systems of a data center.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ pipe system holds pressurized air in the pipes and releases water only when a head opens.\nA ⟦2⟧ system needs a second trigger, such as smoke detection, before water enters the pipes.\n```","dry","pre-action",,,,"wet","deluge","clean-agent","Dry pipe systems keep air in the pipes to reduce the risk of leaks, and pre-action systems add a second trigger before water flows. Wet pipes hold water, deluge systems open every head, and clean agents are not water systems.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.1|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:ports","Fill in each blank with the well-known port number.","```text\nSSH: TCP port ⟦1⟧\nHTTPS: TCP port ⟦2⟧\nDNS: UDP port ⟦3⟧\n```","22","443","53",,,"23","80","25","SSH uses port 22, HTTPS 443 and DNS queries use UDP port 53. Port 23 is Telnet, 80 is HTTP and 25 is SMTP.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:4.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:vpn","Fill in each blank to describe the VPN types and modes.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ VPN connects two sites permanently.\nA ⟦2⟧ VPN connects an individual user's device on demand.\nIPsec ⟦3⟧ mode encrypts the entire original IP packet.\n```","site-to-site","remote-access","tunnel",,,"transport","split-tunnel","full-mesh","Site-to-site VPNs link networks, remote-access VPNs connect users, and tunnel mode wraps the whole original packet. Transport mode protects only the payload, and split-tunnel and full-mesh describe other design choices.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:mfa","Fill in each blank with the authentication factor category.","```text\nA password is something you ⟦1⟧.\nA hardware token is something you ⟦2⟧.\nA fingerprint is something you ⟦3⟧.\n```","know","have","are",,,"do","see","trust","Knowledge, possession and inherence are the classic factor categories. Behavior-based factors are sometimes called something you do, but seeing and trusting are not factors.",1,1
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:access-control","Fill in each blank with the access control model.","```text\nIn ⟦1⟧ the owner of an object decides who can access it.\nIn ⟦2⟧ access depends on assigned job functions.\nIn ⟦3⟧ a central authority labels subjects and objects.\n```","DAC","RBAC","MAC",,,"ABAC","RuBAC","ACL","Discretionary control lets owners decide, role-based control uses job functions, and mandatory control relies on labels set by a central authority. ABAC uses attributes, RuBAC uses rules, and an ACL is a mechanism, not a model.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:5.4|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:abac","Fill in each blank with the component of an access decision architecture.","```text\nThe ⟦1⟧ evaluates an access request against policy and returns a decision.\nThe ⟦2⟧ enforces that decision at the resource.\n```","policy decision point","policy enforcement point",,,,"policy administration point","policy information point","identity provider","The policy decision point decides, and the policy enforcement point applies the decision. The administration point authors policies, the information point supplies attributes, and an identity provider authenticates users.",2,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:testing","Fill in each blank with the testing term.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ lists known weaknesses without exploiting them.\nA ⟦2⟧ attempts to exploit weaknesses to show impact.\nSAST analyzes ⟦3⟧ code without running it.\n```","vulnerability scan","penetration test","source",,,"log review","runtime","binary","A scan identifies weaknesses, a penetration test exploits them, and SAST reads the source code without running it. A log review and runtime testing are different activities.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:6.3|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:kpi|topic:kri","Fill in each blank with the metric type.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ shows how well a process is performing.\nA ⟦2⟧ signals increasing exposure to risk.\n```","KPI","KRI",,,,"SLA","RTO","ALE","A key performance indicator measures performance, and a key risk indicator warns of rising risk. An SLA is an agreement, an RTO is a recovery target, and ALE is an expected loss.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.14|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:physical-security","Fill in each blank with the physical control.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ is a small space with two interlocking doors that lets one person through at a time.\nA ⟦2⟧ is a post that stops vehicles from reaching a building.\n```","mantrap","bollard",,,,"turnstile","fence","gate","A mantrap prevents tailgating with interlocking doors, and a bollard stops vehicles. A turnstile, a fence and a gate do not match these descriptions.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.11|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:metrics","Fill in each blank to describe the recovery metrics.","```text\nMTTR is the average time to ⟦1⟧ a failure.\nMTBF is the average time ⟦2⟧ failures.\nA higher MTBF indicates better ⟦3⟧.\n```","resolve","between","reliability",,,"detect","during","recoverability","Mean time to resolve measures how long a failure takes to fix, and mean time between failures measures how long a system runs before failing again, which indicates reliability. The distractors do not match the definitions.",1,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:7.3|objective:cisco:300-635:2.1|objective:cisco:350-901:1.5|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:configuration-management|topic:iac","Fill in each blank to describe configuration management.","```text\nA ⟦1⟧ is the approved minimum configuration that systems are compared against.\nA difference between a system and that configuration is called ⟦2⟧.\nStoring the configuration in ⟦3⟧ gives history, review and rollback.\n```","baseline","drift","version control",,,"standard","latency","backups","A baseline is the approved reference, drift is a deviation from it, and keeping configuration in version control provides history and review. A standard is a requirement, latency is a delay, and backups do not provide review.",2,2
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.5|objective:cisco:350-901:3.6|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2024|topic:cissp-2027|topic:secure-coding|topic:sql-injection","Fill in each blank so that the query treats the name as data and never as SQL.","```python\nimport sqlite3\n\ndef find_user(conn, name):\n    # Purpose: look up a user by name without allowing SQL injection\n    cur = conn.execute('SELECT id FROM users WHERE name = ⟦1⟧', ⟦2⟧)\n    return cur.fetchone()\n```","?","(name,)",,,,"name","%s","{name}","A placeholder marks where a value goes, and the value is passed separately as a parameter tuple, so the database never parses it as SQL. Putting the name directly into the text, or formatting it in, allows injection.",3,3
  "objective:isc2:cissp:8.2|topic:cissp|topic:cissp-2027|topic:sast|topic:dast|topic:sca","Fill in each blank with the testing technique.","```text\n⟦1⟧ analyzes source code without running it.\n⟦2⟧ tests a running application from the outside.\n⟦3⟧ finds known vulnerable libraries in dependencies.\n```","SAST","DAST","SCA",,,"IAST","SBOM","fuzzing","SAST reads the code, DAST attacks the running application, and SCA matches dependencies against known vulnerabilities. IAST instruments a running application, an SBOM is an inventory, and fuzzing sends malformed input.",1,2
